# Cairn — agent rules

Cairn is an original, local-first native iOS app (Swift + SwiftUI). It is a clean-room
design: no code, strings, assets, iconography, layout, or color identity is copied from
Off Grid AI, Reins, or any other client. Only public, documented wire formats
(Ollama HTTP API, OpenAI-compatible `/v1/chat/completions`) are used, which is interface
compatibility, not copied expression.

## Product invariants (never trade these away)
- Zero subscriptions, paywalls, message caps, telemetry, ads, or accounts.
- Chat history, settings, and attachments stay on the device by default.
- The network is an opt-in peripheral. Anything that leaves the device needs explicit
  user opt-in first and a persistent outbound badge afterwards.
- We do not modify, patch, or bypass paywalls or restrictions in any other app.

## Build & verification loop (authored on an Apple Silicon Mac, verified on macOS CI)
- Decision 2026-11-15: the development machine is a MacBook Pro (Apple Silicon) with Xcode, so
  **local Xcode is the main loop and GitHub CI is the second check**. Run `Scripts/ci.sh` locally
  — that is the same sequence CI runs — and prefer it over waiting for, or polling, a CI run for
  anything you can build on the laptop. Do not poll a CI run to find out whether your code
  compiles; compile it. CI still decides milestones, because it is the environment that is not
  this machine.
- `Scripts/ci.sh` is the source of truth for the sequence: `guards`, `hosts`, `build`, `test`,
  `device`, `all`, `probe`. On a Windows machine with no Xcode, only `guards` runs; that is the
  fallback authoring setup, not the normal path.
- The `.xcodeproj` is generated by `xcodegen generate` and is git-ignored. Never hand-edit
  `Cairn.xcodeproj`; edit `project.yml` instead.
- **Signing is local-only.** A Team ID goes in `Config/Local.xcconfig`, which is git-ignored and
  pulled in by `Config/Signing.xcconfig` through an optional `#include?`. Never put a Team ID, an
  Apple-ID-stamped signing identity, or a pinned provisioning profile in a tracked file, and never
  add `DEVELOPMENT_TEAM` to `project.yml`. `Scripts/check-signing.sh` enforces this. CI has no
  Local.xcconfig and builds unsigned, which is the intended behaviour.
- **One xcconfig, relative includes only.** Both targets point at `Config/Signing.xcconfig`; never add
  a second xcconfig that includes another to share settings, and never write an include as
  `$(SRCROOT)/...` — Xcode does not resolve that path while reading an include, so a plain `#include`
  fails the build (CI run 43794042407) and `#include?` would silently drop the Team ID.
- SwiftPM is for internal module boundaries only. It is never a substitute for the app
  target in the generated `.xcodeproj`.
- Milestone gate = all green:
  1. `Scripts/ci.sh guards` — the privacy guards plus `Scripts/test-guards.sh`, which proves the
     guards still reject planted values and never print what they caught.
  2. `swift test` in `Packages/CairnChatDomain` and `Packages/CairnProviderRemote` (fast, host).
  3. `xcodebuild build` + `xcodebuild test` on `iPhone 17` / iOS 26.5 simulator.
- Local green is not a milestone. A milestone is claimed only with a green CI run number on the
  commit being claimed — CI remains the second check, and both must be cited for a closure.
- Pinned CI: `macos-26` runner, `Xcode_26.6`, SDK ceiling iOS 26.5. The `xcode-27` image is
  public preview and is used by the nightly job ONLY — never as a milestone gate. If the local
  Xcode is newer than the pin, expect local/CI divergence; `Scripts/ci.sh probe` prints both sides.
- Never claim a milestone is done without a green CI run number to cite.
- Never dispatch a `workflow_dispatch` workflow (`ipa.yml`, the nightly probe) yourself. Ask the
  user; they trigger those.

## Module boundaries
- `CairnChatDomain`: Swift 6 strict concurrency, pure. No SwiftUI, no SwiftData, no
  URLSession, no Foundation networking. This is where the tree and the hybrid engine live.
- `CairnPersistence`: Swift 6 strict, SwiftData records + store. Only module allowed to
  touch SwiftData types.
- `CairnAppUI`: Swift 6 strict, SwiftUI views only. Views never call providers or stores
  directly; they go through `ChatSessionModel`.
- `CairnProviderRemote` (Milestone 2): URLSession, SSE, Bonjour, Keychain.
- `CairnInferenceLocal` (Milestone 3): Foundation Models + memory budget + downloads.
- `CairnInferenceNative` (Milestone 3): Swift 5 mode ONLY. A llama.cpp / mlx-swift C++
  interop dependency may not be strict-concurrency-clean, so it is isolated here and
  actor-wrapped at its boundary. Document the reason in the module header.
- Dependency direction is one-way: AppUI -> ChatDomain <- Persistence. Nothing imports AppUI.

## Data rules
- The chat is a TREE. `ChatNode.parentID` + `branchIndex`; a conversation points at
  `activeLeafID`. Edit / regenerate / branch APPEND a sibling and repoint the leaf.
  Never rewrite a flat array, never delete history to "fix" it.
- A Hybrid Synthesized turn is ONE assistant node carrying its candidate drafts, so it
  replays, exports, and imports as a single node.
- Temporary chat mode uses `EphemeralStore` only: never persisted, never cached to disk,
  wiped on process death.

## Inference rules
- One protocol: `InferenceProvider` -> `AsyncThrowingStream<StreamChunk, any Error>` with
  `.thinking` / `.content` / `.toolCall` / `.metrics`.
- Show a reasoning panel ONLY when a backend emits a thinking field or `<think>` tags.
  Never fabricate or paraphrase reasoning that the model did not emit.
- Never hold two on-device models resident unless `os_proc_available_memory()` allows it.
  The synthesizer defaults to the remote model, or runs sequentially.
- `BGContinuedProcessingTask`'s `.gpu` resource: check
  `BGTaskScheduler.shared.supportedResources` at runtime and assume it is absent on iPhone.
  ActivityKit is a progress display only, never a compute strategy.

## Privacy & logging rules (CI-enforced)
- `Scripts/check-logging.sh` fails the build on `print(`, `NSLog(`, or `FileHandle`/`os_log`
  writes in production sources. Prompt text, response text, attachments, and secrets are
  NEVER logged. Log typed metadata only: UUIDs, enums, counts, byte sizes, durations.
- `Scripts/check-signing.sh` fails CI if a real Team ID, an Apple-ID-stamped signing identity, or a
  pinned provisioning profile lands in a tracked file. Team IDs belong in `Config/Local.xcconfig`,
  which is git-ignored — a Team ID is not secret but it is personal and unrotatable.
- `Scripts/test-guards.sh` is the guards' own self-test: it plants values that must be caught in a
  throwaway tree and proves each guard rejects them **and** does not print the value it caught. Run
  it after touching any guard or its allow-list.
- Secrets go to Keychain with `.afterFirstUnlockThisDeviceOnly`. Never in UserDefaults,
  never in a plist, never in a CI log, never in an issue or PR body.
- Every request carries a `NetworkClass`: `.onDevice`, `.privateLan` (RFC 1918, `.local`,
  unqualified, loopback), `.privateVPN` (a mesh-VPN name or the 100.64.0.0/10 block — classified
  honestly, but not part of the plan), or `.publicInternet`.
  `.publicInternet` requires opt-in + badge. Credential-bearing headers
  (`Authorization`, `X-API-Key`, `Api-Key`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `Cookie`) go to Keychain
  only; other custom header values live in SwiftData, are never logged, and the UI warns
  against putting credentials in ordinary headers.
- **Transport decision (2026-11-15, revised the same day).** A server on the user's own network
  is reached over plain `http://` — a LAN IP literal, or that same private address through the
  router's WireGuard tunnel. No mesh-VPN product, no VPN DNS name, no HTTPS requirement: nothing
  beyond the user's own router is part of the plan, and no code may assume one is present.
  Apple's ATS "applies only to connections made to public host names" and gives no protection
  to IP addresses, unqualified host names, or `.local` hosts, so a private address needs no
  exception at all; when away from home the VPN tunnel — not TLS — is what protects the path.
- Exactly one ATS key may ever ship: `NSAllowsLocalNetworking = true`, which covers a `.local`
  or unqualified host *name*. `Scripts/check-ats.sh` fails if it is missing, not true, or
  accompanied by `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads*` or `NSExceptionDomains`. Apple states that key makes
  ATS ignore `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`, so declaring it narrows the app's posture.
- A public-internet profile requires `https://` plus the outbound opt-in. That rule lives in
  code (`NetworkClass.allowsPlaintext` → validation → connection tester) and is unit-tested —
  never enforced by an ATS hole.
- The first request to a LAN address raises the iOS Local Network prompt, and a denial really
  breaks the connection: keep `NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription` and keep the hint that says so.
- No real private hostname **and no real private address** ever enters the repo, commits, tests,
  docs, screenshots, or CI logs — not a VPN DNS name, not a dynamic-DNS name, not a LAN address.
  Placeholders only: `your-host.your-vpn.ts.net` is the single allowed `*.ts.net` value,
  `<your-router-hostname>` in prose, and for addresses only the documented example set that
  `Scripts/check-hostname.sh` allow-lists — `192.168.1.20` and `192.168.1.0/24` for a LAN address
  and subnet, `10.8.0.0`–`10.8.0.2` for tunnel addresses, the `100.64/10` and `172.16/12`
  boundary values used by classifier tests, `127.0.0.1`, `0.0.0.0`. Any other private IP literal
  fails CI, which is what keeps a real LAN or tunnel address from slipping in by accident.
- Plaintext is a warning condition, never a silent one: the UI shows a `Plaintext` badge on any
  profile whose traffic leaves unencrypted.
- **Zero paywall, zero limits (CI-enforced).** `Scripts/check-no-paywall.sh` fails the build on a
  purchase framework, tier or upsell vocabulary, an artificial cap identifier (`maxChat`,
  `chatLimit`, `dailyQuota`, …), or a tracking hook. Scope is what compiles into the binary
  (`Packages/*/Sources`, `Packages/*/Package.swift`, `App`, `project.yml`). Docs, `ROADMAP.md`,
  `.clinerules`, `Scripts/`, test targets and workflow files are out of scope: they must be able to
  name the thing they forbid, and the CI step that invokes the guard spells the forbidden word in
  its own path. Inside scope, **no comment may spell a forbidden word either** — rename the
  identifier, never add an exception here. `maxTokens` / `num_predict` are sampling parameters the
  user sets, not caps on the user, so they are deliberately not matched.
  `Scripts/test-guards.sh` proves the guard still rejects planted values and never prints one.

## Working style
- One milestone at a time. Before a milestone, list the exact files to create/modify and its
  acceptance criteria. After it, run the gate and report results plus known gaps.
- Update `ROADMAP.md` status in the same commit that completes a milestone.
- If a fact about Apple APIs cannot be verified from this machine, say so and gate the code
  behind a runtime check rather than assuming it.

## Working rules (context, files, shell, CI, credentials)

### Context
- Never print whole logs, `.xcresult` bundles, or large files. Use offset/limit reads and
  `grep | head/tail`. On a CI failure, read only the failing step plus the test name and the
  assertion — not the whole log.
- At ~70% of the context window: finish the current step, commit, update `ROADMAP.md`, then
  stop and tell the user to start a new task.

### File size
- Keep Swift files under ~150 lines, one primary type per file. If an edit is rejected for
  size, split the file by type instead of appending in parts.
- After any multi-part write, read the file tail and check brace balance before committing.

### Shell
- On the Mac the shell is zsh and the `Scripts/*.sh` guards plus `Scripts/ci.sh` run natively; that
  is the normal path.
- On the Windows fallback machine the shell is PowerShell and its profile script is blocked: always
  run `powershell -NoProfile`. `$_` and nested quoting get mangled when inlined, so put non-trivial
  logic in a `.ps1` file and run `powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <file>`. A bare
  `>` redirect there writes UTF-16, so pipe through `Out-File -Encoding utf8` instead. Git Bash for
  the `*.sh` guards lives at `C:\Program Files\Git\bin\bash.exe`.
- If output is not captured, rerun once redirecting to a temp file and read it with a bounded read.

### CI
- Build and test locally with `Scripts/ci.sh` first. CI is the second check, not a way to find out
  whether the code compiles.
- Use `gh` at `$env:LOCALAPPDATA\cairn-tools\bin\gh.exe` if it exists and is authenticated;
  otherwise use the unauthenticated REST API (the repo is public).
- A run takes ~5-7 minutes: do useful work meanwhile, or report and stop. Do not poll in a loop.

### Credentials
- Never read, extract, or reuse tokens from the Windows Credential Manager or the git
  credential helper, and never put tokens in a command line. If an authenticated action is
  needed (for example triggering a workflow), ask the user to do it from the GitHub Actions
  page or to authenticate `gh` themselves.

### Commits, memory, verification
- Small commits. `ROADMAP.md` is durable memory: update it after every meaningful step.
  Never mark anything done without a green run.
- Report verification in three separate categories and never merge them:
  **CI-verified**, **on-device-verified**, **real-network-verified**.


- Keep files small and single-purpose. Prefer pure functions in `CairnChatDomain` so
  branching and streaming are unit-testable without a simulator.
- If a fact about Apple APIs cannot be verified from this machine, say so and gate the code
  behind a runtime check rather than assuming it.

## Windows-to-macOS Swift & CI Delegation Rule
- This machine runs Windows and only has `swiftc -parse` (syntax-only checking without Apple
  frameworks like `Security`, `SwiftUI`, or `XCTest` on macOS).
- **Mandatory `ask_gemini` usage.** Whenever macOS CI fails, whenever you edit
  Apple-framework-dependent Swift code (`Packages/CairnProviderRemote`, `Packages/CairnAppUI`,
  `App/`, or `Tests/`), or before pushing Swift changes that cannot be compiled locally on
  Windows, you MUST call the `ask_gemini` MCP tool first.
- Pass the relevant `git diff` or full Swift source/test files to `ask_gemini` and instruct
  Gemini to act as a strict Swift 6 / Xcode macOS compiler and XCTest reviewer — checking for
  `@MainActor` / `Sendable` actor-isolation errors, missing imports, type mismatches, and failing
  test logic.
- Say plainly what the local check could **not** see (stubbed Keychain, deleted `Share/`, deleted
  loopback suites, Darwin-only `URLSessionConfiguration` setters, removed `URLError.posix`) so the
  review is aimed at exactly that blind surface instead of the code that was already compiled.
- Do not waste turns trying to scrape unauthenticated GitHub Actions check-run logs via `curl`
  when they time out or return empty annotations. Read the failing step name once, then delegate.

